{
  "dom_id": "step-q171904",
  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "Let $m\\in N$ and $n\\in N.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\u2200\\,n\\in N,\\,1\\le n.$\n- The truth set of the predicate $(\\,\u2200\\,n\\in N,\\,m\\le n\\,)$ is $N.$\n- $\u2200\\,m,n\\in N,\\,m\\le n.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, notice that the statement \n\n$$\n\u2200\\,m,n\\in N,\\,m\\le n\n$$\n\nfrom option III reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ For all natural $m$ and $n,$ we have $m\\le n.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true, since $1\\le n$ for all natural numbers $n.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For the predicate\n\n$$\nP(m):\\,\u2200\\,n\\in N,\\,m\\le n\n$$\n\nwe note that $P(m)$ is true for some $m\\in N$ and false for others. For example, \n\n$$\nP(1):\\,\u2200\\,n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\n$$\n\nis true. However, \n\n$$\nP(2):\\,\u2200\\,n\\in N,\\,2\\le n\n$$\n\nis false, since $2\u22701.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The sentence \n\n$$\n(\\,\u2200\\,m,n\\in N,\\,m\\le n\\,)\n$$\n\n \ncan be written as\n\n$$\n\u2200m\\in N\\,(\\,{{\u2200n\\in N,\\,m\\le n}_{\u23df}}_{P(m)}\\,)\n$$\n\nwhich is true whenever the inner predicate $P(m)$ is true over the universal set $N.$ But, according to statement II, this is not the case.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
